# Every rule here is stated with the reason it exists, because two different things are being kept
# apart: source, and the evidence a run produces. Line endings are the other half of this and live in
# .gitattributes, because they are a repository decision rather than a machine setting.

# --- Python ------------------------------------------------------------
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]

# The interpreter is pinned in .python-version, so a clone can rebuild the environment without the
# environment itself being committed.
.venv/
venv/

# Secrets. A rented-card run needs a token, and the usual way a repository leaks is the file that
# holds it. `.env.example` is the one file in this shape that is worth keeping, because it records
# which variables a run needs without recording their values.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example

# --- Editor and IDE state ----------------------------------------------
# IDE state is per-machine. A conflict in it is an argument about a window layout rather than about
# the reference. `.idea/` was committed before this rule existed and is now untracked.
.idea/
.vscode/

# --- Run artifacts: evidence, not source -------------------------------
# A gate-scale checkpoint is 3.34 GB per arm and a corpus shard is 200 MB, so the reason to ignore
# these is not only size. A recorded number is evidence only together with the key that produced it,
# and that key lives in the record rather than in the tensor file: committing a checkpoint keeps the
# loss and throws away the claim. Committing the corpus is worse, because the epoch permutation is
# defined by each shard's token count, so a corpus that grows changes what sample index i means and
# invalidates every record written against the previous shape.
#
# Output directories are created on demand by the harness, so nothing here needs a placeholder file.
runs/*
corpus/*

# Extensions as well as directories, so an arm written outside the default root is still ignored.
# There is deliberately no `*.json` rule: the pinned tokenizer file is tracked source, and it is the
# input the reference reads. A fixture that has to be committed anyway is deliberate evidence, and
# `git add -f` is what makes that exception visible instead of implicit.
*.pt
*.safetensors
*.gguf
*.bin
*.npy
*.parquet
*.log

# --- Machine noise -------------------------------------------------------
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
